WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.401
IIIF Kasane: Boxes in a video (annotations on a time and a region) — Opening it from the use case

2
00:00:06.403 --> 00:00:13.203
This is the IIIF Kasane use case page for the Ashinoshiri Dosojin Festival.

3
00:00:13.207 --> 00:00:20.757
It uses a documentary film from the Nagano Prefectural Library, published on Shinshu Digital Commons.

4
00:00:20.759 --> 00:00:31.409
On top of the film, boxes on people found by AI are written as IIIF annotations. Each box appears over the video only at its own time.

5
00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:40.010
The boxes are AI output. But a person checked them by eye, and kept only the boxes that really show a person.

6
00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:42.637
Press Open in Kasane.

7
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:49.242
The video opens, with the Annotations tab in the right panel.

8
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:57.267
IIIF Kasane: Boxes in a video (annotations on a time and a region) — Boxes appear as it plays

9
00:00:57.278 --> 00:01:04.303
The video is on the left, and the list of annotations is on the right. There are twenty-seven.

10
00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:08.731
Each one shows the word Person, and its time in the video.

11
00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:10.859
Let us play it.

12
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:20.010
After five seconds, blue boxes appear: a man in front of the mask, and a person with a camera at the back on the right.

13
00:01:20.014 --> 00:01:27.789
A box shows only for the time written in its annotation, and gives way to the next boxes as the scene moves on.

14
00:01:27.793 --> 00:01:32.293
In this film, the boxes run from five to fifteen seconds.

15
00:01:34.402 --> 00:01:41.027
IIIF Kasane: Boxes in a video (annotations on a time and a region) — Jumping to a time from the list

16
00:01:41.032 --> 00:01:45.732
Click an annotation in the list, and the video moves to its time.

17
00:01:46.877 --> 00:01:54.277
We are now at eleven point five seconds. The box of the annotation you clicked is shown in orange.

18
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.080
It holds the man standing in front of the straw mask.

19
00:01:58.082 --> 00:02:01.382
Let us click one more, at five seconds.

20
00:02:02.481 --> 00:02:12.356
This time the box holds a person with a camera, at the back on the right. Every box in this sample was checked by eye to really show a person.

21
00:02:14.034 --> 00:02:20.684
IIIF Kasane: Boxes in a video (annotations on a time and a region) — Just IIIF annotations

22
00:02:20.685 --> 00:02:26.360
Behind each box is an annotation in IIIF, written in the manifest.

23
00:02:26.361 --> 00:02:30.911
Let us look at the annotation at eleven point five seconds.

24
00:02:30.913 --> 00:02:39.488
Its target names the canvas of the video, with hash t equals for the time, and x y w h equals for the region.

25
00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:49.490
The time runs from eleven point five to twelve point five seconds. The region is in pixels of the video: left, top, width and height.

26
00:02:49.494 --> 00:02:52.469
The body is just the word Person.

27
00:02:52.471 --> 00:03:02.646
The data comes from ArchiveBase Video Search. The boxes on people its AI found frame by frame were rewritten as annotations of this form.

28
00:03:02.646 --> 00:03:19.196
The boxes are AI output. The AI also put person boxes on the straw mask and on shadows. So each of the one hundred and twenty-five boxes was checked against the film, and only the twenty-seven that really show a person were kept.

29
00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:26.899
Write annotations in this form for any other video, and Kasane will lay the boxes over it in the same way.
